      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "certiorari to the united states court of appeals for the eleventh circuit No. 20\u20135904. Argued May 4, 2021\u2014Decided June 14, 2021"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner Terry",
      "case_id": "2020-20-5904",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "94805387e04969a2d76b540582bbb3166bfe423879cc9f24af0f2eae3c16a195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-94805387e049-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Petitioner Tarahrick Terry contends that he is eligible to receive a sentence reduction for his 2008 crack cocaine conviction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-20-5904",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "94805387e04969a2d76b540582bbb3166bfe423879cc9f24af0f2eae3c16a195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-94805387e049-0000-0007",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Act included two base penalties that depended on drug quantity: a 5-year mandatory minimum (triggered by 5 grams of crack or 500 grams of powder) and a 10-year mandatory minimum (triggered by 50 grams of crack or 5 kilograms of powder). 100 Stat. 3207\u20132, 3207\u20133."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-20-5904",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "94805387e04969a2d76b540582bbb3166bfe423879cc9f24af0f2eae3c16a195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-94805387e049-0000-0006",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The District Court sentenced petitioner to 188 months, the bottom of the career-offender Guidelines range."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-20-5904",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "94805387e04969a2d76b540582bbb3166bfe423879cc9f24af0f2eae3c16a195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-94805387e049-0001-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 13,
        "start": 13
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "As enacted in 1986, \u00a7 841(b) created a 100-to-1 ratio between the amounts of powder and crack cocaine necessary to trigger the mandatory minimums in \u00a7\u00a7 841(b)(1)(A) and (B)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-20-5904",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "94805387e04969a2d76b540582bbb3166bfe423879cc9f24af0f2eae3c16a195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-94805387e049-0001-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Under the 1986 law, crack cocaine sentences were about 50 percent longer than those for powder cocaine. USSC, Report to the Congress: Cocaine and Federal Sentencing Policy 13 (May 2007) (2007 Report)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-20-5904",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "94805387e04969a2d76b540582bbb3166bfe423879cc9f24af0f2eae3c16a195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-94805387e049-0001-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Congress eventually responded with the Fair Sentencing Act of 2010. 124 Stat. 2372. Section 2 of the Act increased the amounts of crack cocaine necessary to trigger minimum sentences, reducing the crack-to-powder ratio to 18 to 1."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-20-5904",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "94805387e04969a2d76b540582bbb3166bfe423879cc9f24af0f2eae3c16a195",
      "evidence_id": "ev-94805387e049-0001-0007",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the average sentence reduction under the First Step Act was almost six years. Id., at 43."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
